Q8. What is Hashing?
👉 Hashing is a technique to convert an object (like String, Employee, Student) into a fixed-size integer value called a hash code, 
which is used to quickly store and retrieve objects in data structures like HashMap, HashSet, and Hashtable.

Q9. What is Collision in Hashing?
👉 Collision occurs when two different keys produce the same hash code (same bucket index) in a hash-based collection like HashMap.and are mapped to the same bucket index.
To resolve this, Java uses chaining (LinkedList) and from Java 8 onwards, it uses Red-Black Tree when collisions become frequent.

Q10. Difference between Equals and hashcode method ?
equals():
- It is used to compare two objects for equality.
- By default (from Object class), it compares memory addresses (reference equality).
- But we can override it to compare content of objects (e.g., comparing id or name fields in a class)
hashCode():
- It returns an integer hash value representing the object’s memory address (by default).
- Used mainly in hash-based collections like HashMap, HashSet, Hashtable to decide the bucket location.
- When overridden, hashCode() must generate the same value for objects that are equal according to equals()

Note:- if equals() return true , hashcode() must return the same value.

Q11. suppose You do NOT override equals() and hashCode() ?
👉 Since neither equals() nor hashCode() is overridden, Java uses the default implementations from Object class:
equals() compares object references (p1 == p2)
hashCode() is based on the object’s memory address
✅ Result:
Both p1 and p2 are considered different, even if they contain the same data → both get added to the set.

Q12. suppose You override only equals() but NOT hashCode() ?
🧠 What happens?
equals() says the objects are equal based on data (like name and age)
But hashCode() still gives different hash codes (default from Object)
⚠️ Consequence:
HashSet uses hashCode() first to locate bucket.
Since hash codes are different → it puts them in different buckets → equals() is never even called.
✅ Result:
Still both objects are stored, even though logically they are equal.

Q13. suppose You override only hashCode() but NOT equals() ?
🧠 What happens?
Both objects now have the same hash code (great!)
But equals() still checks reference equality (from Object)
✅ Result:
Java finds the same bucket (due to same hash code)
So both objects get added to the same bucket but as different entries.

Q14. Difference between synchronized collection and concurrent collection ?
Synchronized Collection:
- Old-style collections → Vector, Hashtable, Collections.synchronizedList().
- Whole collection locked → only one thread can access any method at a time.
- Slow in multi-threading because of full lock.
- Iterator is fail-fast → throws exception if modified by another thread.
Concurrent Collection:
- New collections (Java 5+) → ConcurrentHashMap, CopyOnWriteArrayList.
- Partial locking / lock-free → multiple threads can work simultaneously.
- Fast performance in multi-threading.
- Iterator is fail-safe → works on a snapshot, no exception.

Note: Jab tum synchronized collection (jaise Collections.synchronizedList()) use karte ho,tab har ek method (add, remove, get, etc.) 
ek single lock (intrinsic lock) pe dependent hota hai. Matlab agar ek thread add() call kar raha hai, 
toh poore list ka lock hold hota hai → doosra thread koi bhi method (get(), remove()) use nahi kar paayega tab tak.



