Q2. explain internal working of linkedlist ?
 - Datastructure :
                a. LinkedList uses doubly-linked list internally (itself datastructure) where Each element (called a node) contains : Data, Next pointer, Previous pointer 
- Adding elements :
                a. add(element) → adds at the end by updating tail node.
                b. addFirst(element) → adds at head.
                c. addLast(element) → same as add(element).
                d. No shifting required 
- Accessing elements : 
                a. get(index) → must traverse nodes from head or tail to reach index.
                b. Time complexity: O(n) (slower than ArrayList for random access).
- Removing elements :
                a. remove(index) → traverse to node → update previous and next pointers → remove node.
                b. Fast if removing first/last node, slower if removing from middle.
- Memory usage :
               a. Each node stores extra pointers (prev + next), so more memory than ArrayList.

Q3. How many types of linkedlist ?
- Singly → less memory, one-direction.
- Doubly → more memory, both directions.
- Circular → last node connects back to head, useful for circular traversal.


Q4. Is LinkedList synchronized?
👉 No. By default, it is not thread-safe. To make it synchronized →  Collections.synchronizedList(new LinkedList<>());

Q5. How can you use LinkedList as a Queue, Deque, or Stack?
Because LinkedList in Java implements List, Queue, and Deque interfaces, it can behave in multiple ways:
👉 As Queue (FIFO):
- offer() / add() → insert at the tail
- poll() / remove() → remove from head
- peek() → check the head element
👉 As Deque (double-ended queue):
- addFirst(), addLast() → insert at both ends
- removeFirst(), removeLast() → remove from both ends
👉 As Stack (LIFO):
- push() (same as addFirst())
- pop() (same as removeFirst())


Q6. Why LinkedList uses Doubly Linked List instead of Singly?
👉 Because doubly linked list makes:
  - Removal faster (you can directly update prev & next).
  - Traversal possible in both directions.
  - Operations like getLast(), removeLast() efficient.


Q7. Can you reverse a LinkedList in Java?
👉 Yes:
- Using Collections.reverse(list).
- Or manually by traversing nodes and swapping next/prev pointers.


Q8. Write code to remove duplicates from LinkedList.
Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
Iterator<Integer> itr = list.iterator();
while(itr.hasNext()) {
    Integer val = itr.next();
    if(!set.add(val)) {
